Q: Simplifying const Overloading? A: <p>Since C++23, with <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/member_functions.html#Explicit_object_member_functions" rel="nofollow noreferrer">deducing this/Explicit_object_parameter</a>, you might write only:</p>
<pre><code>template &lt;typename T&gt;
class Vector {
public:
    auto&amp; operator[] (this auto&amp;&amp; self, size_t index) {
        // Common implementation, using self, as
        // return self.data[index];
        // ...
    }
    // ...
};
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/T6MarWdWc" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<p>but syntax might be tricky for beginners :-/</p>

Q: Dynamically parsing a string as the name of an object A: <p>Instantiating a class by name can be done like this (below).</p>
<p>But be careful about instantiating a class with a name that came from an untrusted source (otherwise an attacker could cause your server to create a class and potentially run code that you didn't intend). For example, you could prepend a namespace to the class name, and put only classes that are safe to invoked like this within that namespace.</p>
<pre><code>executeRequest(&quot;Class1&quot;,&quot;argument&quot;);


string executeRequest(string requestedClass, object taskDictionary)
{
    IMyHandler objectRef = InstantiateClassByName(requestedClass);
    objectRef.EntryPoint(taskDictionary);
    // do rest of return value stuff here
    return null;
}

/// &lt;summary&gt; Returns an instance of a named class. 
/// The class must have a default constructor and implement IMyHandler.
/// &lt;/summary&gt;
IMyHandler InstantiateClassByName(string className)
{
    Type? clazz = Type.GetType(className);
    if (clazz == null)
        throw new Exception(&quot;Class not found: &quot; + className);
    else
        return (IMyHandler)clazz.GetConstructor(new Type[] { }).Invoke(null);
           // throws exception if class does not have a default constructor
           // or does not implement IMyHandler.
}


/// &lt;summary&gt; Interface that all handlers must implement. &lt;/summary&gt;
public interface IMyHandler
{
    void EntryPoint(object taskDictionary);

}

/// &lt;summary&gt; Example class that could be used. &lt;/summary&gt;
public class Class1 : IMyHandler
{
    public void EntryPoint(object taskDictionary)
    {
        Console.WriteLine(&quot;Class1.EntryPoint() called: &quot; + taskDictionary);
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Automatic install of the backported version of the Android Photo Picker not playing nicely with Android Studio A: <p>The docs weren't correct as reported in <a href="https://issuetracker.google.com/issues/280659181" rel="nofollow noreferrer">this issue</a>.</p>
<p>The recommendation is to add a <code>tools:ignore=&quot;MissingClass&quot;</code> attribute
and to also suppress <code>AndroidDomInspection</code>:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;!--
    Prompt Google Play services to install the backported photo picker module
    https://developer.android.com/training/data-storage/shared/photopicker#device-availability
--&gt;
&lt;!--suppress AndroidDomInspection--&gt;
&lt;service android:name=&quot;com.google.android.gms.metadata.ModuleDependencies&quot;
    android:enabled=&quot;false&quot;
    android:exported=&quot;false&quot;
    tools:ignore=&quot;MissingClass&quot;&gt;
    &lt;intent-filter&gt;
        &lt;action android:name=&quot;com.google.android.gms.metadata.MODULE_DEPENDENCIES&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;/intent-filter&gt;
    &lt;meta-data android:name=&quot;photopicker_activity:0:required&quot; android:value=&quot;&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/service&gt;
</code></pre>
